    "result_text": "Done. The suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing, the only test class in the project).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe app generates `OwnerDto` from `openapi.yml`, and `namesakeCount` is derived on the way out by `OwnerNamesakeCountAdvice`. That advice is the one place where the count is already available \u2014 so the tier rule slots in there naturally with no new class, no new wiring, and no timing/ordering coupling to a second aspect.\n\n**Two files:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `membershipTier` string to the `Owner` schema (generates `getMembershipTier`/`setMembershipTier`).\n\n2. **`OwnerNamesakeCountAdvice.addNamesakeCount`** \u2014 captured the already-computed count into a local, then set the tier:\n   ```java\n   owner.setNamesakeCount(namesakeCount);\n   boolean hasEmail = owner.getEmail() != null && !owner.getEmail().isBlank();\n   owner.setMembershipTier(namesakeCount == 0 && hasEmail ? \"SILVER\" : \"BRONZE\");\n   ```\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** the logic reuses the count already being computed, so it's a two-line addition to one small method (its only sibling, `matches`, is trivial \u2192 low `WMC_other`), and I touched just two files. No new class, no duplicated namesake logic, and the tier is set in the same pass as the field it depends on \u2014 avoiding fragile inter-aspect ordering.",
